    <h2>Japanese Logic Puzzles: A Different Kind of Brain Teaser</h2>

    <p>If riddles are sprints and logic puzzles are jogs, Japanese grid puzzles are hikes: longer, steadier, and with much better views. Publishers like Nikoli, the Tokyo company behind Sudoku, developed a family of puzzles where the entire challenge lives in one clean rule set. There is no vocabulary to know, no trivia to recall, no language barrier at all. Every puzzle is generated so that it has exactly one solution reachable by pure logic, which means you never have to guess, and you never have to wonder whether the puzzle was fair. That combination, total accessibility plus guaranteed fairness, is what makes Japanese logic puzzles such a satisfying breed of brain teaser. You sit down with a grid, you reason your way to certainty, and the finished picture feels earned.</p>

    <h3>Shikaku: The Rectangle Partition Puzzle</h3>
    <p>Shikaku means "divide by square" in Japanese, and the name tells you the whole game. You get a grid with numbers scattered across it, and your task is to draw rectangles so that each number is enclosed by a rectangle containing exactly that many cells, with no overlaps and no empty cells left over. A 6 must claim six cells: 1 by 6, 2 by 3, or 3 by 2, and the neighboring clues quietly tell you which. It starts gentle and becomes genuinely absorbing around the medium levels, when you have to hold several possible partitions in your head at once. Imagine the board as a warm cream grid where every completed rectangle settles into place with a soft cat-colored tint, the whole puzzle slowly assembling like a mosaic. GridPaw's version adds exactly that: playful cats that nap on each rectangle you draw.</p>

    <h3>Akari: The Light Up Puzzle</h3>
    <p>Akari, also known as Light Up, flips the premise. Instead of dividing space, you are lighting it. You place light bulbs on a grid of white and black cells so that every white cell ends up illuminated, while no two bulbs ever see each other in the same row or column unobstructed. Black cells block light and many carry numbers telling you exactly how many bulbs must touch them. Every placement creates ripples across the grid, so the puzzle plays a bit like chess: one bulb placement constrains three or four future decisions at once. Picture a dark grid where each correct bulb throws a warm glowing beam down its row and column, the board brightening cell by cell until the whole puzzle is lit. That is Akari on GridPaw, with a few cat-themed touches along the way.</p>

