    <h2>How to Build a Daily Brain Teaser Habit</h2>

    <p>Here is the good news: you do not need discipline for this, you need a trigger and a small enough first step that saying no feels silly. Puzzle habits are among the easiest habits to build, because the reward is built in. A few practical pointers:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Start with 10 minutes a day.</strong> Attach it to something you already do: your morning coffee, your commute, the wind-down before bed. One logic puzzle or three riddles is a complete session. Ten minutes daily beats an hour once a week, for your brain and for your streak.</li>
      <li><strong>Begin easy, and let difficulty find you.</strong> If a puzzle type feels frustrating rather than fun, drop down a level. The goal is that "click" feeling, and it only shows up when the puzzle is hard enough to engage you but not so hard you stall. Difficulty should rise with your skill, not with your ambition.</li>
      <li><strong>Rotate puzzle types.</strong> Logic on Monday, riddles on Tuesday, a visual challenge midweek, a Shikaku or Akari grid on the weekend. Different puzzles exercise different muscles: deduction, language, perception, spatial reasoning. Rotation keeps the habit fresh and your brain guessing.</li>
      <li><strong>Play with friends.</strong> Share a riddle at dinner, race a partner on the same grid puzzle, or drop a brain teaser into the group chat and watch the theories roll in. Puzzles are more memorable when they come with an argument about the answer.</li>
      <li><strong>Track your streak, not your speed.</strong> The satisfying part is showing up, not being fast. A calendar with check marks does more for a puzzle habit than a stopwatch ever will.</li>
    </ul>

